<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lao People's Revolutionary Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as LPRP, Lao People's Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lao People's Revolutionary Party is the communist party that has ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> as the only legal party since 1975. It was founded in 1955 as the Lao People's Party, with roots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a>, and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a> in the civil war, taking its present name in 1972. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaysone-phomvihane">Kaysone Phomvihane</a> it abolished the monarchy, and it remains closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lao-peoples-revolutionary-party.jpg" alt="Lao People's Revolutionary Party" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sshu94 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LPRP_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The forerunner of the party was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> established in February 1930 to organise a coordinated fighting force against French domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, in cooperation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a>. Though it claimed to be Indochinese, it was dominated by Vietnamese and had no Cambodian or Laotian members before its formal dissolution on 11 November 1945, when the Indochinese National Democratic Revolution was proclaimed.</p><p>In 1946 the underground party began a drive to recruit Laotian cadres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaysone-phomvihane">Kaysone Phomvihane</a>, half Vietnamese, was recruited in 1946-47 while a student at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, but by February 1951 only 81 of its 2,091 members were Lao. The Lao People's Revolutionary Party was established on 22 March 1955 by former members of the Indochinese party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party led the insurgency against the Royal Lao Government and supported North Vietnamese forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>. The insurgency ended with the party securing power in 1975. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaysone-phomvihane">Kaysone Phomvihane</a> was general secretary from 1955 until his death in 1992, yet he was unknown to most citizens in the party's first years in power, when it kept its secretive ways. Voices of the movement before 1975, such as the Lao Patriotic Front chairman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>, were set aside, and independent news organisations were closed or forced into the Lao Front for National Construction, a mass organisation controlled by the party. By 1980 ten percent of the population had left the country.</p><p>The party aimed to abolish the capitalist mode of production and build a planned economy on the Soviet model. In December 1975 it decided to skip the capitalist phase on the way to socialism, while judging that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> was too underdeveloped for immediate socialist construction. It collectivised agriculture from 1978. In the 1980s, influenced by market reforms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, it privatised state companies and legalised private property. By the 1990s it held that the state would use state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> to build the material base of socialism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party's organisation follows democratic centralism, conceived by Lenin: lower organs obey higher ones and internal factions are banned. Its highest body is the National Congress, which meets every five years and elects the Central Committee. The committee elects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>, the Secretariat and the General Secretary, the highest party officer. Article 3 of the constitution guarantees its monopoly on state power. It follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> and Kaysone Phomvihane Thought, and holds that there is no ideological reason for other parties to exist. Its youth league, the Lao People's Revolutionary Youth Union, was established in 1983, and it keeps close relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lao People's Revolutionary Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Lao People's Revolutionary Party</b> rules as the only legal party, closely allied with Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-peoples-democratic-republic">Lao People's Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
